# Introducing Arrow Functions in JavaScript

#### Now that you know how to write basic functions using the function keyword, let’s look at a newer, shorter way to write them.

#### Arrow functions do the same thing as regular functions, but use a simpler syntax.

#### They’re very common in modern JavaScript, especially when working with event handlers or short, single-purpose functions. 

---

##🧩 1. Start with the same idea — a function that does something

#### Here’s a basic function we’ve seen before:

<img src="https://images.code.org/866bbb37e2cb602dcdbe4226ceed22a3-image-1762380706476.png" alt="add five funciton in javascript" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

#### This works perfectly,but in JavaScript, we can write the same thing using an arrow function.

---

## 🔁 2. Replace the function keyword with arrow syntax

#### Instead of function addFive() {}, we write:

<img src="https://images.code.org/cd57942c79b818039772e5a007da3435-image-1762380917289.png" alt="add five funciton in javascript" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">
<div style="clear:both;"></div>


#### ✅ **What’s happening here:**

- #### We use const to store the function in a variable named addFive.

- #### The parentheses () come before an arrow =>.

- #### The curly braces { } hold the same instructions as before.

#### It’s the same logic — just a shorter, modern way to define a function.

---


## 💡 3. Named arrow functions can still be reused

#### If you plan to call the same function in multiple places, give it a name — just like you did with regular functions:

<img src="https://images.code.org/4d6ab12d022993a607bcaaa4cb34b4b9-image-1762381088234.png" alt="add five funciton in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">
<div style="clear:both;"></div>



#### This makes your code neat, readable, and consistent with other functions in your app.

---

## Now you try!

#### In the next level, you will use AI to help connect an inline event handler to a reusable, named arrow function. 

